      ],
      "id": "34q10pr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aa1i16d",
      "content": "Sound is a form of energy that produces the sensation of hearing. Sound is produced due to vibration of objects."
    },
    {
      "id": "4tkddvf",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "0y5ajh2",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_0y5ajh2.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yzieral",
      "content": "Sound always requires a medium to travel and cannot travel in vacuum."
    },
    {
      "id": "c72te6i",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PRODUCTION OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3y2ytpk",
      "content": "Sound is produced when an object vibrates."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qsycowj",
      "content": "These vibrations produce sound waves which travel through a medium."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fokf7co",
      "content": "Examples:"
    },
    {
      "id": "x765v2w",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Vibrating guitar string</li><li>Ringing of bell</li><li>Human vocal cords</li></ul>",
        "Vibrating guitar string",
        "Ringing of bell",
        "Human vocal cords"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "9fwgzth",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SOUND WAVES"
    },
    {
      "id": "at75gif",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "76basr7",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_76basr7.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f9rnkbr",
      "content": "Sound travels in the form of waves called sound waves. Sound waves are: <img></img>"
    },
    {
      "id": "f8kapcu",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Mechanical waves</li><li>Longitudinal waves</li></ul>",
        "Mechanical waves",
        "Longitudinal waves"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6kvtp23",
      "content": "Sound cannot travel in vacuum because particles are required for transmission."
    },
    {
      "id": "0smrb3b",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "LONGITUDINAL WAVES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v26yvzs",
      "content": "In longitudinal waves, particles of the medium vibrate back and forth in the same direction as the wave travels."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ezm23vm",
      "content": "Sound waves consist of:"
    },
    {
      "id": "0mxdupy",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Compression – region of high pressure</li><li>Rarefaction – region of low pressure</li></ul>",
        "Compression – region of high pressure",
        "Rarefaction – region of low pressure"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lrr7tv9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "IMPORTANT TERMS OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6wtiuef",
      "content": "Wavelength (λ):"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wxof6vq",
      "content": "Distance between two consecutive compressions or rarefactions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vog0nzl",
      "content": "Frequency (f):"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8tw0b55",
      "content": "Number of vibrations per second. Unit = Hertz (Hz)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jlg51g0",
      "content": "Time Period (T):"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5xrlbj3",
      "content": "Time taken to complete one vibration."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uu460lc",
      "content": "T = 1 /f"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ke516s3",
      "content": "Amplitude:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "50et710",
      "content": "Maximum displacement of particles. Loudness depends on amplitude."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CHAPTER 7 : SOUND",
      "id": "20zqhi9"
    },
    {
      "id": "wgx640e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SPEED OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7stthf7",
      "content": "Speed of sound depends on:"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8lztu9",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Medium</li><li>Temperature</li><li>Humidity</li></ul>",
        "Medium",
        "Temperature",
        "Humidity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "giwfowd",
      "content": "Speed of sound:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sdfrcsx",
      "content": "Air – 343 m/s Water – 1500 m/s Steel – 5000 m/s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zsywdik",
      "content": "Sound travels fastest in solids and slowest in gases."
    },
    {
      "id": "yqxwhji",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "CHARACTERISTICS OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sd7thq3",
      "content": "Loudness:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8modbtn",
      "content": "Depends on amplitude and measured in decibel (dB). <img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g78r8z4",
      "content": "Pitch:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "insaxdz",
      "content": "Depends on frequency."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wtwh1ky",
      "content": "Higher frequency → higher pitch."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9tbca5c",
      "content": "Quality:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qgwypsz",
      "content": "Helps to differentiate between sounds of same pitch and loudness."
    },
    {
      "id": "tv8zcwf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "AUDIBLE RANGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yyvt24j",
      "content": "Human ear can hear sound between 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz. Below 20 Hz – Infrasonic"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "juyreu0",
      "content": "Above 20,000 Hz – Ultrasonic"
    },
    {
      "id": "f1il69x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ECHO"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "exy1fve",
      "content": "Echo is repetition of sound caused by reflection. Conditions for echo:"
    },
    {
      "id": "w66p4qz",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Minimum distance = 17 metres</li><li>Time gap = 0.1 second</li></ul>",
        "Minimum distance = 17 metres",
        "Time gap = 0.1 second"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cza5ejj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SONAR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e10sz5z",
      "content": "SONAR stands for Sound Navigation And Ranging. Uses:"
    },
    {
      "id": "38fk0vr",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Measuring sea depth</li><li>Detecting submarines</li><li>Locating underwater objects<img></img></li></ul>",
        "Measuring sea depth",
        "Detecting submarines",
        "Locating underwater objects<img></img>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qc0y49c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "HUMAN EAR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ewjun8x",
      "content": "The human ear has three parts:"
    },
    {
      "id": "2hbjijb",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Outer ear – collects sound</li><li>Middle ear – amplifies vibrations</li><li>Inner ear – converts vibrations into nerve signals</li></ul>",
        "Outer ear – collects sound",
        "Middle ear – amplifies vibrations",
        "Inner ear – converts vibrations into nerve signals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ksmvvwk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "NOISE POLLUTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pp6o7l3",
      "content": "Noise is unwanted or unpleasant sound."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cn6pnms",
      "content": "Causes:"
    },
    {
      "id": "ej48d1p",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Vehicles</li><li>Machines</li><li>Loudspeakers</li></ul>",
        "Vehicles",
        "Machines",
        "Loudspeakers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "04k23yn",
      "content": "Effects:"
    },
    {
      "id": "u9p1z6s",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Hearing loss</li><li>Stress</li><li>High blood pressure</li></ul>",
        "Hearing loss",
        "Stress",
        "High blood pressure"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Frequently tested: Mathematical calculations based on Echo and SONAR principles.",
        "High yield topic: The human audible range of sound frequencies and parameters affecting speed of sound.",
        "Key conceptual area: Distinguishing characteristics of sound (pitch, amplitude, and quality)."
      ],
      "id": "mt3fo2i"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l8gdoce",
      "content": "Control:"
    },
    {
      "id": "37qugfv",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Use of silencers</li><li>Plantation</li><li>Soundproofing</li></ul>",
        "Use of silencers",
        "Plantation",
        "Soundproofing"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ts7tydn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "IMPORTANT  QUESTIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gv9rt05",
      "content": "Q1. Unit of frequency?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yusatld",
      "content": "Ans: Hertz"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a2kry12",
      "content": "Q2. Sound travels fastest in? Ans: Solids"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z8aer3h",
      "content": "Q3. Audible range of human ear? Ans: 20 Hz – 20,000 Hz"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "48vu03j",
      "content": "Q4. Full form of SONAR?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "t9wuz41",
      "content": "Ans: Sound Navigation And Ranging"
    },
    {
